.TH "ENCODING AND DECODING FUNCTIONS FOR PSTRINGS." "3" "" "pstring 1.5" "pstring manual"
.SH NAME
Encoding and decoding functions for pstrings. \- The pstring library supports encoding and decoding many common formats out of the box.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
\fB#include <encoding.h>\fR
.fi
.PP
.nf
\fBtypedef int(pstrenc_fn)(pstring_t *dst, const pstring_t *src);\fR
.fi
.PP
.nf
\fBint pstrenc(pstring_t *dst, const pstring_t *src, const char *enc);
int pstrdec(pstring_t *dst, const pstring_t *src, const char *enc);\fR
.fi
.PP
.nf
\fBint pstrenc_hex(pstring_t *dst, const pstring_t *src);
int pstrdec_hex(pstring_t *dst, const pstring_t *src);\fR
.fi
.PP
.nf
\fBint pstrenc_url(pstring_t *dst, const pstring_t *src);
int pstrdec_url(pstring_t *dst, const pstring_t *src);\fR
.fi
.PP
.nf
\fBint pstrenc_base32(pstring_t *dst, const pstring_t *src);
int pstrdec_base32(pstring_t *dst, const pstring_t *src);\fR
.fi
.PP
.nf
\fBint pstrenc_base64(pstring_t *dst, const pstring_t *src);
int pstrdec_base64(pstring_t *dst, const pstring_t *src);\fR
.fi
.PP
.nf
\fBint pstrenc_base64url(pstring_t *dst, const pstring_t *src);
int pstrdec_base64url(pstring_t *dst, const pstring_t *src);\fR
.fi
.PP
.nf
\fBint pstrenc_base64table(
    pstring_t *dst, const pstring_t *src, const pstring_t *table
);
int pstrdec_base64table(
    pstring_t *dst, const pstring_t *src, const pstring_t *table
);\fR
.fi
.PP
.nf
\fBint pstrenc_cstring(pstring_t *dst, const pstring_t *src);
int pstrdec_cstring(pstring_t *dst, const pstring_t *src);\fR
.fi
.PP
.nf
\fBint pstrenc_utf8(pstring_t *dst, const uint32_t *src, size_t length);
int pstrdec_utf8(uint32_t *dst, size_t *length, const pstring_t *src);\fR
.fi
.PP
.nf
\fBint pstrenc_utf16(
    pstring_t *dst, const uint32_t *src, size_t length, int endian
);\fR
.fi
.PP
.nf
\fBint pstrdec_utf16(
    uint32_t *dst, size_t *length, const pstring_t *src, int endian
);\fR
.fi
.PP
.nf
\fBint pstrenc_json(pstring_t *dst, const pstring_t *src);
int pstrdec_json(pstring_t *dst, const pstring_t *src);\fR
.fi
.PP
.nf
\fBint pstrenc_xml(pstring_t *dst, const pstring_t *src);
int pstrdec_xml(pstring_t *dst, const pstring_t *src);\fR
.fi
.PP
.nf
\fBPSTR_INLINE int pstrenc_html(pstring_t *dst, const pstring_t *src);
PSTR_INLINE int pstrdec_html(pstring_t *dst, const pstring_t *src);\fR
.fi
.PP
.nf
\fBconst char *pstr_read_utf8(
    const char *chr, const char *end, uint32_t *out
);\fR
.fi
.PP
.nf
\fBchar *pstr_write_utf8(char *out, uint32_t c);\fR
.fi
.PP
.nf
\fBconst char *pstr_read_utf16(
    const char *chr, const char *end, uint32_t *out, int endian
);\fR
.fi
.PP
.nf
\fBchar *pstr_write_utf16(char *out, uint32_t c, int endian);\fR
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
The \fBpstring\fR library supports encoding and decoding many common
formats out of the box. Since strings are assumed to be UTF\-8 encoded,
the encoding functions are divided into two groups:
.IP \(bu 2
pstrenc \- for encoding UTF\-8 to a particular format.
.IP \(bu 2
pstrdec \- for decoding from a particular format to UTF\-8.
.PP
Both of those groups use the same function prototype, where the contents
of parameter \fBsrc\fR are encoded and concatenated to parameter \fBdst\fR:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
int pstrenc_*(pstring_t *dst, const pstring_t *src);
.fi
.RE
.PP
This way of handling parameters allows for encoding a stream of data.
.PP
Many operations on pstrings are encoding\-agnostic, while others assume
an ASCII or UTF\-8 encoding. For those, UTF\-16 support is not explicitly
supported and pstrings need to be converted to UTF\-8 for them to work.
.SH REFERENCE
.SS pstrenc_fn
.PP
Encoding/decoding function prototype.
.SS pstrenc
.PP
Encodes/decodes \fBsrc\fR using the requested encoding format.
.PP
\fBErrors\fR
.PP
EINVAL, ENOMEM, EENCODE, EDECODE.
.SS pstrenc_hex
.PP
Encodes/Decodes the bytes from \fBsrc\fR as a string of hexadecimal numbers.
.PP
For decoding, the source string should have an even length and contain only
hexadecimal digits ('0123456789abcdefABCDEF').
.PP
\fBErrors\fR
.PP
EINVAL, ENOMEM, EENCODE, EDECODE.
.SS pstrenc_url
.PP
Encodes/decodes \fBsrc\fR as a URL compatible string.
.PP
\fBErrors\fR
.PP
EINVAL, ENOMEM, EENCODE, EDECODE.
.SS pstrenc_base32
.PP
Encodes/decodes RFC 4648 base32 (the \fBA\-Z2\-7\fR alphabet, \fB=\fR padding).
Unlike base64, base32's alphabet avoids visually ambiguous characters
(no \fB0\fR/\fBO\fR, \fB1\fR/\fBI\fR/\fBl\fR) and is case\-insensitive on decode, which is
why it shows up in things meant to be typed by hand: TOTP/2FA secret
keys, DNSSEC/DANE record encodings, and Crockford\-style IDs (though
Crockford's own variant uses a different alphabet, not this one).
.PP
\fBErrors\fR
.PP
EINVAL, ENOMEM, EENCODE, EDECODE.
.SS pstrenc_base64
.PP
Encodes/decodes \fBsrc\fR as a Base64\-encoded string.
.PP
\fBErrors\fR
.PP
EINVAL, ENOMEM, EENCODE, EDECODE.
.SS pstrenc_base64url
.PP
Encodes/decodes \fBsrc\fR as a URL\-safe Base64\-encoded string.
.PP
\fBErrors\fR
.PP
EINVAL, ENOMEM, EENCODE, EDECODE.
.SS pstrenc_base64table
.PP
Encodes/decodes \fBsrc\fR as a Base64\-encoded string using the provided
translation table. \fBtable\fR must be exactly 64 characters long.
.PP
\fBErrors\fR
.PP
EINVAL, ENOMEM, EENCODE, EDECODE.
.SS pstrenc_cstring
.PP
Encodes/decodes an escaped version of \fBsrc\fR into \fBdst\fR, which, if surrounded
by quotes, is safe to use as a string literal in C source code.
.PP
\fBErrors\fR
.PP
EINVAL, ENOMEM, EENCODE, EDECODE.
.SS pstrenc_utf8
.PP
Encodes/decodes codepoints from \fBsrc\fR as UTF8 characters.
.PP
For decoding variant, parameter \fBlength\fR should point to the maximum length
of the buffer \fBdst\fR, which will be changed by the function to the number of
codepoints decoded.
.PP
\fBErrors\fR
.PP
EINVAL, ENOMEM, EENCODE, EDECODE.
.SS pstrenc_utf16
.PP
Encodes codepoints from \fBsrc\fR as UTF\-16 code units into \fBdst\fR, using
\fBendian\fR byte order. Codepoints above \fB0xFFFF\fR are encoded as a
surrogate pair (four bytes); a codepoint in the surrogate range
(\fB0xD800\fR\-\fB0xDFFF\fR) or above \fB0x10FFFF\fR is invalid UTF\-16 and is
replaced with the replacement character \fBU+FFFD\fR, matching how
\fBpstrdec_utf8\fR already handles invalid input rather than failing.
.PP
\fBErrors\fR
.PP
EINVAL, ENOMEM, EENCODE, EDECODE.
.SS pstrdec_utf16
.PP
Decodes UTF\-16 code units from \fBsrc\fR (byte order \fBendian\fR) into
Unicode codepoints in \fBdst\fR. \fBlength\fR should point to the capacity of
\fBdst\fR in codepoints (not code units); it is updated to the number of
codepoints actually decoded. An unpaired or out\-of\-order surrogate
decodes to \fBU+FFFD\fR and consumes just the one code unit, so decoding
never gets permanently stuck on malformed input.
.PP
\fBErrors\fR
.PP
EINVAL, ENOMEM, EENCODE, EDECODE.
.SS pstrenc_json
.PP
Encodes/decodes \fBsrc\fR as a JSON string into \fBdst\fR.
.PP
\fBErrors\fR
.PP
EINVAL, ENOMEM, EENCODE, EDECODE.
.SS pstrenc_xml
.PP
Encodes/decodes \fBsrc\fR as a XML string into \fBdst\fR.
.PP
\fBErrors\fR
.PP
EINVAL, ENOMEM, ENOENT, EENCODE, EDECODE.
.SS pstrenc_html
.PP
Encodes/decodes \fBsrc\fR as a HTML string into \fBdst\fR.
.PP
\fBErrors\fR
.PP
EINVAL, ENOMEM, ENOENT, EENCODE, EDECODE.
.SS pstr_read_utf8
.PP
Reads a UTF\-8 character and stores it in \fBout\fR if it's not \fBNULL\fR.
Returns a pointer to the first byte after the read character.
.SS pstr_write_utf8
.PP
Writes a UTF\-8 character and stores it in \fBout\fR, returning the
end of the written byte sequence. The output buffer should
be big enough to store at least 4 bytes.
.SS pstr_read_utf16
.PP
Reads one UTF\-16 code unit sequence (one code unit, or a surrogate
pair) starting at \fBchr\fR and stores the decoded codepoint in \fBout\fR if
it's not \fBNULL\fR. Returns a pointer to the first byte after the
sequence read, which is always \fBchr + 2\fR (a lone/invalid code unit)
or \fBchr + 4\fR (a valid surrogate pair).
.SS pstr_write_utf16
.PP
Writes codepoint \fBc\fR as one or two UTF\-16 code units (four bytes for
a surrogate pair), returning the end of the written sequence. The
output buffer should be big enough to store at least 4 bytes. A
codepoint that cannot be represented in UTF\-16 (in the surrogate
range, or above \fB0x10FFFF\fR) is replaced with \fBU+FFFD\fR.
